# kfmt: Kairo formatting spec

This file is the formatter's spec. Edit it freely; the code follows it.

How to read the tags on every rule:

- No tag: a rule fixed in review.
- `(proposed)`: a default proposed for review: accept, change, or delete.
- `[impl]`: `Extras/Formatter/Driver/kfmt.k` does this today.
- `[partial]`: some of it is implemented; the rule says which part is missing.
- `[spec-only]`: not implemented yet.

Open questions (conflicts or undecided points) are listed in section 17,
each with its readings; none was resolved silently.

Proposed defaults come from how `Compiler/**/*.k` is
written today (the house style) and from the language reference
(https://kairolang.org/docs/language/). Where those two disagree, the
rule says so.

Examples use ```rs fences, as in the draft. "Before" is what someone wrote;
"after" is what kfmt prints.

The user-facing page, https://kairolang.org/docs/toolchain/kfmt/, is written (0.2.0) and summarises this spec; it was frontmatter
only today. It gets written in full once the formatter is complete, from this
spec; until then this file is the only description of kfmt's rules.


## 1. Principles

**1.1 Output parses.** (proposed) [partial] kfmt never writes a file that
does not parse. Today a file with a syntax error is refused before
formatting starts; the output is not re-parsed.

**1.2 Meaning never changes.** (proposed) [partial] The output parses to the
same AST as the input. The only differences allowed are whitespace, line
breaks, redundant `;` (section 13), trailing commas (rule 4.2), the order and form of imports
(section 5), and, under `--normalize-parens` only, redundant parentheses
(rule 9.5). Today kfmt checks a weaker property, see 1.5 and section 16.

**1.3 Comments are never lost, never reordered.** (proposed) [impl] Every
comment in the input is in the output, with its text unchanged and in the
same order relative to other comments. A comment may move relative to code
(rule 12.4); it never moves past another comment.

**1.4 Idempotent.** (proposed) [partial] `kfmt(kfmt(x)) == kfmt(x)`. Several
rules depend on how the source was written (one-line blocks, enum trailing commas,
parentheses, alignment runs on consecutive lines). Each such rule is written
so its own output keeps the property that triggered it. Section 16 lists
them. No golden test checks this yet.

**1.5 Write-back is gated.** (proposed) [impl] `--write` replaces a file only
when the output passes the round-trip check. Today that check is: the output
re-lexes to the same code tokens, the same non-blank text between tokens, and
the same comments in the same order. Line breaks are not code tokens, so the
check does not catch a line break that changes parsing (see 4.5 and 16.3).

**1.6 Text inside literals is never touched.** (proposed) [impl] String,
char, raw-string and f-string contents (including the expressions inside
`{...}` of an f-string) are copied byte for byte. A multi-line string or
char literal pins its continuation lines to column 0: re-indenting them would
change the value.

**1.7 When in doubt, copy.** (proposed) [impl] A construct kfmt does not
know how to lay out is copied as written and only re-indented to its new
nesting level. Section 15 lists what is copied today.


## 2. Configuration

The formatting knobs are exactly the ones below (2.2-2.5, 2.8). Everything else
is fixed. There is no config file and no `--blankline` flag.

**2.1 Line width: 100 columns, fixed.** [impl] Not a knob
(`Formatter::line_width()`).

**2.2 `--no-align`.** [spec-only] Turns off every alignment rule
(section 11, and the aligned list form of rule 4.1, which then falls back to
the block form). Default: alignment on.

**2.3 Indent kind: spaces or tabs.** [spec-only] Default spaces.
Flag spelling (proposed): `--indent=spaces|tabs`. Leading indentation in the
input, tabs or spaces, is rewritten in the chosen kind. Tabs are only ever
indentation: the padding that aligns columns (section 11, rule 4.1's aligned
form) is always spaces, after the tabs of the indent, also in tabs mode
. Alignment therefore survives any tab width in an editor. For the
width limit (2.1, 4.1), a tab counts as `--tab-size` columns.
> Tabs outside leading indentation (between tokens) collapse with the rest of
> the inter-token whitespace (rule 3.3); tabs inside literals and comments
> stay. (proposed) [partial]

**2.4 `--tab-size=N`.** [partial] Width of one indent level: N spaces
in spaces mode (default 4, Kairo's tab size), N tabs in tabs mode (default 1).
Today the level is fixed at 4 spaces (`indent_width()` in Renderer.k).

**2.5 `--normalize-parens`.** (flag name proposed) [spec-only] Default
off. Turns on rule 9.5's parenthesis normalization. The compiler tree's
format pass turns it on.

**2.6 Blank-line cap: 4, fixed.** [impl] Not a knob; see rule 14.1.

**2.8 `--call-width=N`.** [impl] Where a call's one-line
form must end (rule 4.1). Default 80; N from 40 to the line width (100).
The compiler tree's format pass (Compiler/, Linker/, Extras/Formatter/) uses
`--call-width=100`: calls there get the full line width like every other
list.

**2.7 Modes.** (proposed) [impl] Not formatting knobs: `--check` (default:
list files that would change, exit 1 if any), `--write` (rewrite in place),
`--stdout` (print the formatted file). A directory argument formats every
`.k` file under it.


## 3. Whitespace and indentation

**3.1 Indentation is structural.** (one level = `--tab-size`) [impl]
Each `{ }` body is one level deeper than the line holding the `{`. Wrapped
lists in the block form (rule 4.1) are one level deeper than the line
holding the opener.

**3.2 No trailing whitespace; blank lines are empty.** (proposed) [impl]

**3.3 One space between tokens where a space belongs, none elsewhere.**
(proposed) [partial] What "belongs" is set per construct in sections 6-10.
Today only the parts kfmt lays out from the AST are respaced; headers and
types are copied as written (section 15), so `if  x  {` keeps its spaces.
```rs
// before
var x=a+ b *c
if  x>0{ f( a ,b ) }
// after
var x = a + b * c
if x > 0 { f(a, b) }
```

**3.4 File ends with exactly one newline.** (proposed) [partial] Leading
blank lines at the top of the file are removed.

**3.5 Line endings are LF.** (proposed) [spec-only] CRLF input becomes LF.
> Alternative: keep the file's own line ending. Pick one.


## 4. Line breaking and wrapping

**4.1 Parenthesized, bracketed and braced lists: all on one line, or one
item per line.** [partial] Applies to parameter lists, call
arguments, tuple literals, list literals `[...]`, named initializers
`T { ... }` and braced import lists. If the list does not fit on one line,
every item goes on its own line. There is no "fill as many as fit" mode.

The limit: 100 columns, except a call, whose one-line form must end
by column **80**; a call that would cross column 80 is split. "Call" means any
call expression: free function, method, constructor, `Type::f(...)`
. Tuple and list literals, named initializers, parameter lists
and imports use 100. The call limit is `--call-width=N` (2.8); the compiler
tree's format pass sets it to 100, other code keeps 80.

The two broken forms follow LLVM / Google clang-format:
- **Aligned form.** The first item stays on the opener's line, right after
  `(` / `[` / `{`; the other items align to its column (padding in spaces,
  2.3); the closer follows the last item on its line.
- **Block form.** No item on the opener's line; the items go one level deeper
  than the line holding the opener; the closer goes on its own line at that
  line's indent.

Which form each list gets:
- Function parameters: always aligned; the first parameter stays on the
  `fn f(` line.
- Call arguments, tuple and list literals, named initializers: always block.
- Imports: always block (rule 5.2).
- Long name lists in imports and in list / tuple / set / map literals: the
  grid form below instead of one per line, when it applies.
- Fallback for parameters: if the aligned column leaves too little room (the
  widest parameter would still cross the width), use the block form.
  (proposed)
- With `--no-align`, parameters use the block form too. The grid
  form is alignment as well, so under `--no-align` those lists are one item
  per line. (proposed)

**Grid form ("block table").** A block-form list of short items puts
several items per row, column-aligned: each column is padded with spaces to
its widest item.
```rs
import foo::{
    Bar,  Baz,   Foo,
    Sazs, Ianhs, Achoo,
}
```
- Where: imports and list / tuple / set / map literals. Not call
  arguments, parameters or named initializers. A `case` with many
  alternatives has its own grid, rule 8.4.
- When: the list has more than 6 items, no item is over 24
  columns, no item spans several lines, and no comment sits between items.
  Otherwise one item per line.
- Columns per row: as many as fit within
  100 columns at the list's indent, counting every column as wide as the
  widest item (plus `, `), at most 8. The rows are then padded per column,
  as in the example, so a column can be narrower than that estimate.
- Order: items keep their order, row by row; imports are
  sorted first (5.2).
- The last row may be short; it is not padded at its end.

Aligned form:
```rs
fn example(item1,
           item2,
           item3,
           ...
           itemLast)
```
Block form, a call over 80 columns (trailing comma, rule 4.2):
```rs
// before
diag->report(DiagID::R049E, name_range, f"unknown name '{name}\x27 in this scope")
// after
diag->report(
    DiagID::R049E,
    name_range,
    f"unknown name '{name}\x27 in this scope",
)
```
The tree already writes calls this way, for example
`Extras/Formatter/Driver/kfmt.k` in `_seq` (the `Doc::group(Doc::concat4(`
call, arguments one level in, `))` on its own line) and in `main`
(`Logger::set_dump_path(` / one argument / `);`).

When a split call's argument is itself a call that is still too long, that
inner call splits the same way, one level deeper. (proposed)

Today kfmt prints only the block form (`_seq` in kfmt.k), against the single
100-column limit; the aligned form and the 80-column call limit are
[spec-only].

The first draft's text ("aligned to the column just after the opening
paren") is this rule's aligned form; its example (items indented 4, `)` at
3 spaces) is withdrawn.

**4.2 Trailing comma: by form.** (following the grid example)
[spec-only] A list in block or grid form ends with a trailing `,`; a list on
one line has none. kfmt adds or removes it to match. The aligned form
(parameters) has none: its `)` follows the last item. Enum bodies keep their
commas as written (they are member lists, not rule-4.1 lists). Today kfmt
keeps trailing commas exactly as written [impl].
> Never where the comma changes meaning (proposed): a one-element tuple
> `(x,)` keeps its comma on one line, and no comma is added that would turn
> a parenthesized expression into a tuple. Both are token changes, so the
> round-trip check must allow them (16.4).

**4.3 Empty list: `()`, `[]`, `{ }`.** (proposed) [impl] Empty parens and
brackets have no space inside; an empty block is `{ }` with one space.

**4.4 Where a break may go.** (proposed) [impl, as a constraint] Inside `( )`
and `[ ]` a newline is insignificant to the parser (it ignores newlines there),
so kfmt may break anywhere between tokens. Inside `{ }` (and at file scope) a
newline ends a statement, and kfmt may break only where the parser continues
the statement on the next line:
- after `(`, `[`, `,`, or any binary operator at the end of a line;
- before `.`, `->`, `?.`, `?->` (member access continues across a newline;
  `::` does not) [Compiler/Parser/ExprParse.k: "newline continuation: only the
  member-access family"];
- before an infix operator that cannot also start a statement: `&&`, `||`,
  `and`, `or`, `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`, `/`, `%`, `|`, `^`, `<<`,
  `>>`, `??`, `in`, and the compound assignments other than `=` [same file,
  the Pratt loop; the excluded set is `is_unary_operator` in
  Compiler/Token/TokenKind.k];
- before `->` in a function signature, before `derives` / `impl` in a type
  head, before `where` / `requires` on a declaration;
- before `else` and `=>` in an `if` expression, before `catch` in a `try`
  expression.

**4.5 Never break before `+ - * & ~ ! ++ -- .. ... = ? @ as`.**
[impl, by omission] A line starting with one of these starts a new statement,
so `a\n- b` is two statements. kfmt never puts a line break in front of them
outside brackets.
```rs
// never this: it is `var t = a` followed by the statement `-b`
var t = a
    - b
// this, or wrap the right side in ( )
var t = a -
    b
```

**4.6 Binary expressions: break BEFORE `&&` / `||`.** (house style)
[partial] A chain of one logical operator (`&&` or
`||`) that does not fit breaks before every operator, each continuation
indented one level. The parser accepts a line starting with `&&` / `||`
(rule 4.4). Today this is the only binary expression kfmt breaks; all others
stay flat (and may overflow the width).
```rs
if is_constructor(f) && f->params.size() == 1 && is_self_type(f->params[0]) && (!f->is_deleted) {
// after
if is_constructor(f)
    && f->params.size() == 1
    && is_self_type(f->params[0])
    && (!f->is_deleted) {
```
> Difference from the docs (the docs page is
> not edited here): control-flow.mdx, "Line breaks" under `if` expressions,
> says "break after an operator, not before it". That holds for
> `+ - * &` and the rest of the rule-4.5 set, not for `&&` / `||`. A
> suggested wording: "break after `+`, `-`, `*`, `&` (a line starting with
> one of them starts a new statement); `&&` and `||` may start a line".
>
> Other operators (proposed) [spec-only]: when a non-logical binary chain does
> not fit, break after the operator of lowest precedence, continuation
> indented one level. A rule-4.5 operator is always broken after, never
> before.

**4.7 Chained calls.** (proposed) [spec-only] Today a member chain is printed
flat. Proposed: a chain with three or more calls that does not fit breaks
before each `.` / `->` (never before `::`), one step per line, indented one
level from the chain's start.
```rs
var n = self.ctx->symbols().lookup(name).filter(is_visible).first_or_null()
// after, when over the width
var n = self.ctx->symbols()
    .lookup(name)
    .filter(is_visible)
    .first_or_null()
```

**4.8 Long strings are never split.** (proposed) [impl] A string literal
that alone overflows the width stays on one line, overflowing. kfmt never
splits a literal or joins two.

**4.9 Over-wide lines are allowed when nothing can break.** (proposed) [impl]
A line with no legal break point (rule 4.4) is printed as is.

**4.10 Named initializers.** (proposed) [partial] `Type { a: 1, b: 2 }` keeps
one space inside the braces when flat; when it does not fit, one field per
line in rule 4.1's block form, with the `name: value` columns aligned
(rule 11.3). Today: block form, no column alignment.
```rs
var c = Cmd { ind: 0, brk: true, doc: doc, lit: "" }
```


## 5. Imports

**5.1 One name in braces: drop the braces.** [spec-only]
```rs
// before
import Foo::{foo}
// after
import Foo::foo
```
A single aliased item drops the braces too (`import std::io::println
as pl` was checked with the release compiler, and imports.mdx now shows the
form):
```rs
// before
import Foo::{Bar as Baz}
// after
import Foo::Bar as Baz
```
> Also (proposed): `import Foo::{*}` becomes `import Foo::*`, and a single
> nested path `import Foo::{list::LinkedList}` becomes
> `import Foo::list::LinkedList` (imports.mdx: items resolve relative to the
> base).

**5.2 Two or more names: always broken.** [spec-only] Even when the
list would fit on one line. Up to 6 names (or any long name): rule 4.1's
block form, one per line. More than 6 short names: the grid form (rule 4.1).
Either way the list ends with a trailing comma (revising the
earlier "no trailing comma" call to match the grid example, rule 4.2) and
there is no `;` after the closing `}` (section 13).
```rs
// before
import Foo::{foo, bar};
// after
import Foo::{
    foo,
    bar,
}

// before
import foo::{Foo, Bar, Achoo, Baz, Sazs, Ianhs}
// after (6 names: still one per line)
import foo::{
    Achoo,
    Bar,
    Baz,
    Foo,
    Ianhs,
    Sazs,
}
```
With a seventh name the same list takes the grid form, sorted, row by row.
The language docs write `import math::{Vector3, Matrix4}` on one line; that
is a difference from this rule, not an error in the docs.

Item order inside the braces (proposed) [spec-only]: sorted the same way as
5.4. Nested paths (`list::LinkedList`) sort by their full text.

**5.3 FFI header imports first.** [spec-only] Every `ffi "..." import`
line comes first, then one blank line, then the Kairo imports.
```rs
// before
import std::io
ffi "c++" import "Native/KShims.hh"
import AST::Node::Base
ffi "c++" import "cstdlib"
// after
ffi "c++" import "Native/KShims.hh"
ffi "c++" import "cstdlib"

import AST::Node::Base
import std::io
```
FFI imports keep their relative order. (proposed) C and C++ headers can
depend on what an earlier header defined, so reordering them can break the
build.

**5.4 Kairo imports sorted by path.** [spec-only] Key: the path text
after the `import` keyword and any `pub`/`priv`/`prot`/`module` word, compared
byte by byte (so uppercase sorts before lowercase, as in `Sema.k` today).
(proposed: the exact key)
> Proposed details:
> - Visibility does not group: `pub import` lines sort among the rest. (Or
>   group `pub` first? Pick one.)
> - Only a contiguous run of imports is sorted. A comment line or a blank
>   line inside the run is a barrier: imports above and below it sort
>   separately, and the comment stays where it was. (Otherwise a section
>   comment such as `// sema passes` would detach from its group.)
> - Imports inside `eval if` branches, `module` bodies and other blocks are
>   sorted within that block, never moved out of it.
> - A trailing comment travels with its import line.
> - Exact duplicate imports are left alone (removing one is a lint, not
>   formatting).

**5.5 Spacing.** (proposed) [spec-only] `import a::b::{x, y}` has no space
around `::`; one space after `,` in a flat list; `as` has one space on each
side.

Today kfmt copies every import as written (section 15), and the round-trip
check (1.5) rejects any reordering. Rules 5.1-5.4 need the check relaxed to
"same set of imports" (section 16). Rules 5.3-5.4 always apply; there is no
flag to turn sorting off (2.2-2.5 are the only knobs).


## 6. Declarations

**6.1 Function head.** (proposed) [partial] One line when it fits:
`fn name(params) modifiers -> Ret {`. One space before `{`. `->` has one
space on each side. In a parameter, `name: Type` has no space before `:` and
one after. Generic parameters attach to `fn`: `fn <T> name(...)`, one space
after `fn` and none inside `<...>`. Today only the parameter list is laid out
(rule 4.1); the rest of the head is copied as written.
```rs
fn <T>pick( x :T,n:i32 )->i32{ return 1 }
// after
fn <T> pick(x: T, n: i32) -> i32 { return 1 }
```

**6.2 Wrapped parameters.** (aligned form, type column) [partial]
Parameters wrap in rule 4.1's aligned form, `:` glued to each name, and the
types align in one column (rule 11.1). `) -> Ret {` follow the last parameter
on its line (proposed).
```rs
fn create_session(config: *SessionConfig, diag: *DiagnosticsEngine, sources: *SourceManager, opts: *Options) -> *Session {
// after
fn create_session(config:  *SessionConfig,
                  diag:    *DiagnosticsEngine,
                  sources: *SourceManager,
                  opts:    *Options) -> *Session {
```
Today kfmt prints the block form with `)` on its own line and no column.

**6.3 `where` and `requires`.** (proposed) [spec-only] On the head's line
when the whole head fits; otherwise on their own continuation line, indented
2 spaces, with `{` at its end. This is the language docs' style
(where.mdx, requires.mdx).
```rs
fn safe_index(arr: [i32], i: i32) -> i32 requires i >= 0 && i < arr.length() {
// after, when over the width
fn safe_index(arr: [i32], i: i32) -> i32
  requires i >= 0 && i < arr.length() {
    return arr[i]
}
```
> The 2-space continuation is the only non-4 indent in this spec. The other
> choice is 4. Pick one.

**6.4 Modifiers keep their written order.** (proposed) [impl] `pub inline fn`
and `fn ... inline` mean the same (functions-declarations.mdx); kfmt keeps
whichever position the author used.

**6.5 Attributes.** (proposed) [impl, copied] A declaration attribute
(`@deprecated`, `@add_logging`, `@copy`) stays on the line it was written on:
its own line above the declaration, or before the keyword on the same line.
kfmt does not move attributes between those two forms: a line break after an
`@name` changes what it attaches to (attributes.mdx). An expression attribute
(`@create Config(8080)`) is part of its expression and never gets a line
break after it.

**6.6 Generics.** (proposed) [impl, copied] `vec::<Doc>`, `cast::<T>(x)`,
`Foo<T>` are copied as written. kfmt never adds spaces around a `<` or `>`
that touches a neighbour, because it may be a generic the parser read as a
comparison.

**6.7 Variables and constants.** (`:` glued) [impl] `var name: Type =
init`: no space before `:`, ever; one after; one space each side of `=`.
Inside an alignment run, rule 11.1 adds padding after the `:`, never before.

**6.8 Function bodies of the form `= expr`.** (proposed) [impl] `fn f(x: i32)
-> i32 = x * 2` stays on one line; when too long, it breaks after `=` with the
expression indented one level (proposed, [spec-only]).

**6.9 Forward declarations.** (proposed) [impl] A declaration with no body
(`fn f() -> i32`, `class Foo`) is copied as written, minus a redundant `;`
(section 13).


## 7. Types: class, struct, union, interface, enum, extend, module

**7.1 Head and brace.** (proposed) [impl] `class Name derives Base impl I {`
on one line, `{` at the end after one space. Members one level in, `}` alone
on the last line. An empty body is `class Empty { }`.
> When the head does not fit, `derives` / `impl` move to a continuation line
> (the parser accepts that) indented 2 spaces, as in 6.3. (proposed)
> [spec-only]

**7.2 Member order is never changed.** (proposed) [impl] Fields, methods,
nested types and comments come out in source order.

**7.3 Blank lines between members: as written, capped (rule 14.1).**
(proposed) [impl] kfmt neither adds nor removes blank lines between members.
> Alternative (proposed, [spec-only]): force one blank line between two
> methods with bodies, the house-style habit. A rule that adds lines, so
> listed separately.

**7.4 Enums.** (proposed) [impl] One variant per line, each followed by `,`
as written (a trailing `,` on the last variant is kept or left absent as
written, rule 4.2). Variants with explicit values on consecutive lines align
their `=` (rule 11.1):
```rs
enum FileMode derives u8 {
    Read = 1 << 0,
    Write = 1 << 1,
    Execute = 1 << 2,
}
// after
enum FileMode derives u8 {
    Read    = 1 << 0,
    Write   = 1 << 1,
    Execute = 1 << 2,
}
```
Variants with payloads are copied as written.

**7.5 One-line type bodies.** (proposed) [partial] `struct Node { var value:
i32 }` written on one line stays on one line while it fits. Today this works
for blocks (rule 8.1) but not for type bodies, which are always broken.
[spec-only for type bodies]

**7.6 `pub { }` visibility groups and `module` blocks** are laid out like
type bodies. (proposed) [impl]


## 8. Statements

**8.1 Blocks always break.** [impl] `{` ends the line
holding the header, one space before it; statements one level in; `}` alone
at the header's indent. Never kept on one line, not even for one simple
statement that would fit (LLVM / Google clang-format's
`AllowShortBlocksOnASingleLine: Never`). An empty block stays `{ }` (4.3).
Type bodies are not blocks: 7.5 still keeps a one-line one.
```rs
if want_tp { time_enable(true, want_tt); }
// after
if want_tp {
    time_enable(true, want_tt)
}
```
Switch: `Rules.blocks_always_break`. Off, the rule this replaced: a block
written on one line with exactly one simple statement and no comment stays
on one line while it fits (and an if/else chain only as a whole,
`Rules.if_chain_together`).

**8.2 `if` / `else if` / `else`.** [impl] `} else {` and
`} else if c {` on one line. Headers are copied as written today, so the
condition's inner spacing is not normalized ([partial]). An `@likely` /
`@unlikely` hint between `}` and `else` stays where it was written.
```rs
if a {
    f()
}
else {
    g()
}
// after
if a {
    f()
} else {
    g()
}
```

**8.3 `if` / `try` expressions.** [impl] Both stay on
one line while they fit; past the column limit they split one clause per
line. The parser takes a line break before `else` / `else if` in an `if`
expression and before `catch` in a `try` expression (`_skip_nl_if_next` in
Compiler/Parser/ExprParse.k), and kfmt's tree check re-parses every output.
- `if`: every clause starts at the `if`'s column, the keywords
  right-aligned so the conditions line up; the last `else` has no condition
  and starts the column. With no `else if` there is nothing to line up and
  `else` goes under `if`.
  ```rs
       if a == b => 1
  else if a == 1 => 2
  else => 3
  ```
  In a statement the `if` keeps its place after `=` / `return`, so the
  padding sits there: `var x =      if a == b => 1`.
- `try`: each `catch` starts in the `try`'s column, and the `=>` column is
  aligned across the arms (a section 11 table).
  ```rs
  var n = try parse_config_file(path, opts)
          catch e: ParseError => -1
          catch e: IoError    => -2
          catch               => 0
  ```
Only the `=>` forms are laid out; a `try` whose arm heads span lines, or
whose operand or a value is itself broken over lines, is copied as written.
An arm too long for its line overflows (or, when it is a call, splits by
4.1); there is no break before `=>`. Switches: `Rules.if_expr_layout`,
`Rules.if_expr_align` (off: the clauses after the first go one level in),
`Rules.try_expr_layout`.

The old `a if c else b` form has no rule: Stage 1 has no infix `if`
(`_infix_precedence` has no `KwIf`). kfmt's leftover layout for it
(`_ternary` in kfmt.k) is to be deleted; see section 18.

**8.4 `match`.** [impl] `match x {`, each
case's block laid out by rule 8.1. A block arm (`case` or `default` with a
block body) chains onto the `}` of the block arm before it: `} case B
{`, `} default {`. A value arm (`case A => 1`) is not a block: one per line.
A comment line between two arms keeps them apart, and a trailing comment on
an arm's `}` line too (it would land after the chained clause); one on the
arm's first line stays there.
```rs
match item.get_kind() {
    case TopLevelKind::Decl {
        self.dispatch_decl(item.as_decl())
    } case TopLevelKind::Stmt {
        self.dispatch_stmt(item.as_stmt())
    }
}
```
A `match` expression with more than 2 cases always breaks:
`match a {`, one `case` per line one level in, `}`. With 2 or fewer it is
copied as written.
```rs
var x = match a {
    case 1 => 10
    case 2 => 20
    default => 0
}
```
**Grid for many alternatives.** A `case` with more than 6
alternatives lays them out like the import grid (4.1): the first row right
after `case `, the other rows aligned under the first pattern, each column
padded to its widest `pattern,`, as many columns as fit within 100 columns
(at most 8, as for imports; no per-item width cap, unlike 4.1's 24), source
order row by row, only the last row short, then ` {` (or a `where` guard,
or ` =>`) after the last pattern.
```rs
case TokenKind::KwIn,                TokenKind::OpLessLessEqual,
     TokenKind::KwAs,                TokenKind::OpGreaterGreaterEqual,
     TokenKind::OpQuestionDotStar {
```
Six or fewer alternatives, and any header holding a comment, a macro or a
multi-line pattern, are copied as written. Switches: `Rules.chain_clauses`,
`Rules.match_expr_break`, `Rules.case_grid`. Aligning the `{` of
consecutive one-line cases (11.6) is moot under 8.1.

**8.5 Loops.** (proposed) [impl] `for x in xs {`, `while c {`, `loop {`.
C-style `for`: `for init; cond; step {`, `;` followed by one space, none
before it. With an empty part, the `;` stays and no space is left where the
part would be (the header is copied as written today, [partial]):
```rs
for var i: usize = 0; i < n; ++i {
for ; i < n; ++i {
for ;; {
```
> `for ; cond; step` is being added to the language now. `for ;; {` is a
> guess at the all-empty spelling; delete it if the grammar does not allow
> it.

**8.6 Generators.** (proposed) [impl] `fn f() -> yield i32 {`; `yield x` is
an ordinary statement.

**8.7 `unsafe` blocks.** (proposed) [impl] `unsafe {` like any block header.

**8.8 `eval if` and `defined!()`.** (proposed) [impl, copied] Today an
`eval if` / `eval` block is copied as written, re-indented as a whole. The
parser may not build a tree for a branch the preprocessor drops (for example
a `defined!(_WIN32)` branch on Linux), so kfmt cannot lay that branch out.
> Proposed [spec-only]: lay out a branch whose contents parse; copy any
> branch that does not. The header `eval if defined!(X) {` follows rule 8.2.

**8.9 `return`, `break`, `continue`, `panic`, `yield`.** (proposed) [impl] One
space between the keyword and its operand or label.

**8.10 Labels.** (proposed) [impl] `outer: for ...` keeps the label on the
loop's line: `name: ` then the statement.

**8.11 `finally`, `try`/`catch` statements, context bindings.** [impl] Header as written, block by 8.1, `} catch ... {` on
one line, and a `finally` right after a statement `try` chains onto its last
`}`: `} finally {`. It is still the scope-exit `finally`
(diagnostics.mdx: a `finally` on the line of the `try`'s closing brace or
the next is the same statement), so the move changes nothing.

**8.12 One statement per line.** ("newlines where needed") [spec-only]
Two statements on one line separated by `;` are split onto two lines, and
the `;` goes (section 13). Except: a one-line block keeps its one statement
(8.1); a `for` header keeps its `;`s.
```rs
var a = 1; var b = 2
// after
var a = 1
var b = 2
```


## 9. Expressions

**9.1 Binary operators: one space each side.** (proposed) [impl] Except the
member-pointer operators `.*` `->*` `?.*` `?->*`, which take no spaces, and
`<` / `>` / `>>` touching a neighbour (rule 6.6).

**9.2 Unary operators: no space.** (proposed) [impl] `-x`, `!ok`, `*p`, `&x`,
`++i`, `i++`. Word operators keep one space: `not x`, `await f()`,
`spawn t`, `thread x`, `eval x`, `unsafe &x`.

**9.3 Casts.** (proposed) [impl] `x as T`, one space each side of `as`.
Casts in any other mode (`CastMode` other than `Default`) are copied as written.

**9.4 Ranges.** (proposed) [impl] `a..b`, `a..=b`, no spaces.

**9.5 Parentheses: kept by default, normalized under `--normalize-parens`.**
 Default (flag off) [impl]: kfmt never adds or removes a parenthesis.

With `--normalize-parens` [spec-only], kfmt rewrites the parentheses of
binary and unary operator expressions by Stage 1 precedence: a pair that does
not change how the expression parses is removed; a pair the parse needs is
kept; kfmt never adds one. The output must pass the AST-equality check
(16.3): same operator tree, only redundant parens gone.
```rs
// before (Stage 0 workarounds)
if (!a) && b { (*p) += x }
var t = (a * b) + c
var u = !(a == b)
// after --normalize-parens
if !a && b { *p += x }
var t = a * b + c
var u = !(a == b)
```
This is meaning-preserving only under Stage 1 precedence. Stage 0 parses
`!a && b` as `!(a && b)` and `*p += x` as `*(p += x)`, so a file that Stage 0
still has to compile must not be formatted with the flag on. The compiler
tree's format pass turns it on (the tree is Stage 1 source).

Details (proposed):
- Only parens whose content is a binary or unary operator expression (or
  whose parent is one) are candidates. Parens around an `if` / `try`
  expression, a statement expression `({ ... })`, a tuple, a closure, a
  cast, a call's argument list, or a control-flow condition are never
  touched.
- A pair a diagnostic asks for stays, even if the parse would not change:
  `base + (if c => 1 else => 2)` (S078W), nested `if` arms (S077E).
- A pair holding a comment stays.
- A pair that keeps a `<` apart from a `>` stays: a depth-0 `<` inside it
  with a `>` (`>>`, `>=`, `>>=`) later in the statement, or a depth-0 `>`
  inside it with a `<` earlier. `(xs[k].imp < 0) || f > (g)` keeps its pair:
  without it the parser reads `imp<0 || f>(g)` as a generic call
  (tentative generic arguments after a member step or an unknown name).
- Equal precedence: a pair is redundant only when associativity gives the
  same tree, so `a - (b - c)` keeps its pair and `(a - b) - c` loses it.

**9.6 Calls and subscripts.** (proposed) [impl] `f(a, b)`, `xs[i]`: no space
before `(` / `[`, none inside, one after each `,`. Named arguments:
`name: value`, `:` glued to the name; when the argument list is broken one
per line, the values align in one column (rule 11.3).

**9.7 Closures.** (proposed) [impl, copied] `fn (a: i32) -> i32 { ... }` and
`fn ()|x, *y| { ... }`: one space between `fn` and `(`; the capture list
`|...|` attaches to `)`. The head (`fn`, generics, parameters, captures,
`-> T`) is copied as written. [impl] A block
body is laid out like any block: 13.1 drops its redundant `;`, 9.5 applies,
and a comment or macro use inside it belongs to the body's statement list, so
it no longer makes the call or statement around the closure copied. A body
written on one line with exactly one statement (no comment, no macro) stays
on one line while it fits, `op(at, fn () { s->spell(at, p) }, t)`, instead
of the three lines 8.1 would give each such argument; any other body is
broken by 8.1. An expression body (`fn (x) => x`) is still copied.

**9.8 f-strings and literals.** (strings untouched) [impl] Copied byte
for byte, including the `{...}` parts and number spellings (`0xFF` stays
`0xFF`, `and` stays `and`).

**9.9 Address-of.** (proposed) [impl] `&x`, `unsafe &x`, `&null`; never `& x`.

**9.10 Statement expressions.** (proposed) [impl, copied]
`({ var t = load(); (t.value); })` is copied as written; its `;`s are
required (rule 13.2).


## 10. FFI blocks and declarations

**10.1 `ffi "c++" { ... }` blocks** are laid out like type bodies (section 7).
(proposed) [impl]

**10.2 `ffi "c++" fn f(i32) -> *Window`** follows rules 6.1-6.2. (proposed)
[partial]

**10.3 `ffi "..." import "header"`** lines: see 5.3. The header string is a
literal and is never changed.


## 11. Alignment

**11.1 Assignment runs align their `=`.** [impl] From the first
draft:
```rs
item1      = 67
item2      = 34
itemscount = 56
tree       = "binary"
```

**11.2 Declaration runs align keyword, name and type columns; `:` is always
glued to the name.** [partial] Across a run of consecutive
declarations, mixed keywords included, the keyword column is padded to the
longest keyword, and the type column starts one space past the longest
`name:`:
```rs
// before
var i1: u32
const i2: u32
static i3: u32
eval i4: u32
var itemscount: u8
// after
var    i1:         u32
const  i2:         u32
static i3:         u32
eval   i4:         u32
var    itemscount: u8
```
Where the declarations also have initializers, `=` aligns as a fourth column
(proposed); rows without an initializer leave it empty:
```rs
pub var kind:     DocKind    = DocKind::Concat
pub var txt:      string     = ""
pub var children: vec::<Doc>
```
The "keyword" column is everything before the name: `var`, `pub var`,
`static const` (proposed). This is the house style of the compiler tree, so
Compiler/ field blocks mostly keep their shape. The first draft's
`var item1      : u32` form (padding before `:`) is withdrawn.

The same columns apply to wrapped parameters (rule 6.2) and, through 11.3, to
named arguments and named initializer fields.

Today kfmt aligns one column with the padding before `:` (the withdrawn
form), and only rows that all use the same separator; it has no keyword or
second column.

**What a run is.** (impl, kept) Consecutive rows on consecutive source lines
(no blank line, no comment line between), each row on one line, of the same
kind (assignments with `=`; declarations; enum values with `=`), each row
fitting the width at its indent. A run needs at least two rows. Compound
assignments (`+=`) and rows holding a comment or macro do not align. Untyped
`var x = v` rows join a declaration run with an empty type column (proposed).

**11.3 Name/value lists align when broken.** [spec-only] When a
parameter list, a named-argument list or a named initializer is broken one
per line, the `name: value` (or `name: Type`) rows align their value column,
`:` glued to the name. When the list is flat, no padding.
```rs
var c = Cmd {
    indent: 0,
    brk:    true,
    doc:    some_long_document_expression(),
    lit:    "",
}
```
(The value column is one space past the longest `name:`, here `indent:`.)

**11.4 Trailing comments in a run align.** (proposed) [spec-only] Consecutive
lines that each end with a trailing comment align the comments one space past
the longest line, if that stays within the width. Today a trailing comment
gets exactly one space before it, so the house-style column (`Line,
///< ...` in DocIR.k) is lost.
```rs
Text,
Line,          ///< a space flat, a newline broken
HardLine,      ///< always a newline, at the current indent
```
> Alternative: at least 2 spaces before a trailing comment, the house habit.

**11.5 Nowhere else.** (proposed) Positional arguments, list and tuple
items, and `case` arms do not align beyond rule 4.1's aligned form.

**11.6 Same-shape one-liners.** (proposed) [spec-only] Align the `{` of
consecutive one-line overloads, as where.mdx does
(`... where x > 0  { return 1 }`). No flag of its own (2.2-2.5 are the only
knobs): accept it as always-on under alignment, or delete it.

All of section 11 is off under `--no-align` (2.2).


## 12. Comments

**12.1 Text is never changed.** (proposed) [impl] No re-wrapping, no
re-spacing inside `//`, `///`, `///<` or `/* */`.

**12.2 Own-line comments** are re-indented to the indent of the code they sit
in, and keep their blank-line spacing (rule 14.1). (proposed) [impl]

**12.3 Doc comments.** (proposed) [impl] `///` stays on its own lines above
the declaration; `///<` stays trailing after its member. The file header
banner (`/// --- The Kairo Project --- ///`) is an ordinary comment block and
is never reflowed.

**12.4 Trailing comment relocation.** [impl] From the first draft:
because reformatting changes line counts, a trailing comment is re-anchored
to the line where its associated node starts, not where it originally sat:
```rs
fn example(item1, item2, item3, ...itemLast)   // hello mate
```
becomes, once wrapped:
```rs
fn example(item1,   // hello mate
           item2,
           item3,
           ...
           itemLast)
```
The comment stays on the node's first line. With rule 4.1's
aligned form that line also holds the first parameter, so the text and the
example now agree. A comment trailing a multi-line element stays on that
element's last line (kfmt does this today). The code already puts the
comment on the first line; only the aligned list layout is missing.

**12.5 Comment on an opener's line stays there.** (proposed) [impl] In
`class Foo { // why`, the comment stays after `{`.

**12.6 Comment-only blocks.** (proposed) [impl] A block holding only
comments stays a block, comments one level in. (Stage 0 could not compile an
`if` whose body is only comments; Stage 1 can.)

**12.7 Comments inside expressions.** (proposed) [impl] An expression,
declaration head or initializer holding a comment is copied as written, so
the comment cannot drift to the wrong operand.


## 13. Semicolons

Statements end at a newline; `;` is an optional terminator (StmtParse.k,
`_consume_terminator`). A block resets the parser to statement mode even
inside `( )`, so a newline ends a statement inside a closure body or a
statement expression as well (StmtParse.k ~185, ExprParse.k
`_finish_stmt_expr`).

**13.1 Redundant `;` is removed.** [spec-only] A `;` is redundant, and
kfmt removes it, when the next thing after it is a newline, a comment and then
a newline, `}`, or end of file, AND the first token of the next line does not
continue the statement (rule 4.4: `.`, `->`, `?.`, `?->`, a non-ambiguous
infix operator, `->` under a fn head, `derives`/`impl`/`where`/`requires`,
`else`, `catch`, `=>`). If it would continue, the `;` stays: removing it would
join the two lines.
```rs
// before
var x = 1;
f(x);
class Point { var x: i32; var y: i32; };
// after
var x = 1
f(x)
class Point { var x: i32; var y: i32 }
```
(The one-line class keeps its middle `;`; see 13.2. If that body is broken by
rule 7.5, the `;` goes.)

**13.2 Required `;` stays.** (with the cases from the parser) [impl]
- between two statements or members on one line (rule 8.12 splits most of
  these; what remains are one-line type bodies and statement expressions);
- in a C-style `for` header: `for var i = 0; i < n; ++i`, `for ; i < n; ++i`;
- in types: `[i32;]`, `[T; N]`;
- inside a statement expression, `({ var t = load(); (t.value); })`.

**13.3 Never added.** (proposed) kfmt never inserts a `;`.

The whole Compiler/ tree ends statements with `;` today, so turning 13.1 on
changes nearly every line of it. The round-trip check (1.5) compares `;` as a
code token, so 13.1 needs the check relaxed (section 16).


## 14. Blank lines

**14.1 Keep up to 4.** [impl] Preserve up to 4 consecutive blank
lines from source; collapse anything beyond that down to 4. Not
configurable: the draft's `--blankline` flag is dropped (2.6).

**14.2 None at the start or end of a block.** (proposed) [impl] Blank lines
right after `{` or right before `}` are removed.
```rs
fn f() {

    g()

}
// after
fn f() {
    g()
}
```

**14.3 None added.** (proposed) [impl] kfmt adds a blank line only where
rule 5.3 needs one (between the ffi and Kairo imports) and where 14.4 does.

**14.4 A blank line after a closer line.** [impl] After a
line consisting only of closers (`}`, `)`, `]`, optionally followed by `;`
or `,`; a trailing comment does not count) one blank line goes before the
next line, unless that line also begins with a closer (`}`, `} else {`,
`} catch E {`, `)`, ...). Statement level only: between the statements of
a block or file, the members of a type and the arms of a match, never
inside a broken argument or element list (a `),` line followed by the next
argument gets nothing). Never more than one is added, none when a blank
line is already there (14.1's cap holds), none before a block's `}` or at
the end of the file. A comment line after the closer counts like code: it
gets the blank line.
```rs
kairo::Logger::set_dump_path(
    dump_directory,
    file_name,
)

kairo::Logger::dump_to_file()
```
Whether a call broke into a `)` line is the renderer's decision, so the
renderer adds the line (`DocKind::GapLine`). An alignment run (section 11)
ends before a call row that will break, since the blank line after it would
split the run on the next pass. Switch: `Rules.blank_after_closer`.


## 15. What kfmt copies as written today

(proposed: keep this list as the spec until each item gets a rule) [impl]

Copied byte for byte, then re-indented as a whole: control-flow headers
(`if`, `while`, `for`, `match` scrutinee, `catch`), types, patterns, closure
heads (9.7), macro invocations and everything a macro expanded to, imports,
type aliases, macro declarations, `eval` blocks (an `eval if` / `eval for`
statement keeps only its header as written; its blocks are laid out by 8.1),
asserts, `label!` / `jump!`, inline blocks, operator function heads, any
construct holding a comment or a macro use (outside a closure body it holds
whole, 9.7; a declaration that merely contains a macro use is laid out),
chains with generic arguments or operator steps, `<` / `>` glued to a
neighbour, non-default casts, dotted or headless initializers, `if` / `try`
expressions not in the `=>` form (8.3), `match` expressions with 2 cases or
fewer (8.4), multi-line string and char literals (pinned to column 0).

**15.1 Never touched under any rule.** (proposed) Literal contents (1.6),
comment text (12.1), the spelling of numbers and word operators, parentheses
(except under `--normalize-parens`, 9.5), member and import order except
rule 5.3-5.4.


## 16. Golden tests and the safety check

**16.1 Golden pairs.** (proposed) [spec-only] One test per rule: `name.k`
(input) beside `name.expected.k`. The test runs `kfmt --stdout` and diffs.
Each rule's before/after example above is its first test case.

**16.2 Idempotency.** (proposed) [spec-only] Every test also runs kfmt on
the expected file and requires no change. A whole-tree run on Compiler/ and
Lib/ followed by a second run must change nothing.

**16.3 AST equality.** (proposed) [spec-only] Every test parses input and
output and requires the same tree (as `--emit=ast` prints it, locations
ignored), modulo: removed `;` (13.1), import order and form (section 5),
and parentheses the parser folds away (9.5). This is the check that catches
a line break that changes parsing (4.5) and a paren removal that changes the
operator tree (9.5), neither of which today's token-only check (1.5) can see.
Every `--normalize-parens` test runs it with the flag on.

**16.4 Relaxing the write-back check.** (proposed) [spec-only] The 1.5 check
must ignore `;` tokens that 13.1 removed, trailing commas 4.2 adds or drops, and
(under `--normalize-parens`) removed parens, and compare imports as a set
(plus the brace rewrites of 5.1-5.2). Until it does, those rules cannot ship:
today any output that differs by a `;`, a `,`, a paren or an import's
position is refused. Paren removal additionally needs the 16.3 tree check at
write time, since a token comparison cannot tell a redundant pair from a
needed one.

**16.5 Refusal is not failure.** (proposed) [impl] A file kfmt cannot format
safely is left untouched and reported as "refused" (exit 1); the others
are still written.


## 17. Open questions

O-1 to O-11 were answered and folded into rules 2.x, 4.1,
4.6, 4.10, 5.1, 5.2, 8.3, 11.1-11.3 and 12.4. The six later rulings are folded into 8.1-8.4, 8.11 and 14.4.

Open (not decided):

- Whether the import and literal grids (4.1) also drop their 24-column item
  cap, as the `case` grid (8.4) does. Kept as is.
- The `case` grid keeps the import grid's 8-column maximum; the ruling says
  only "as many columns as fit". A list of short patterns (`case 1, 2, ...`)
  is the only place it shows.
- 8.3 with no `else if`: `else` under `if` (nothing to line up), and the
  `if`'s padding after `= ` / `return ` (the ruling's example starts at
  column 0).

The remaining "Pick one" notes inside proposed rules (3.5 line endings,
5.4 `pub` grouping, 6.3 2-space vs 4-space continuation,
7.3 blank lines between methods, 11.4 comment spacing, 11.6) are review
items on proposals, not conflicts.


## 18. Implementation to-dos

Status: kfmt is a Stage 1 build target (`python3 build.py
--debug kfmt`; in build.py's TOOLCHAIN and the staged tree) and implements
the spec below. The `[impl]` / `[partial]` / `[spec-only]` tags in sections
1-16 predate this pass; read this section for what is done.

Done:

- `_ternary` and its call are deleted (O-8); `if c => a else => b` has
  rule 8.3's layout instead.
- Safety (1.1-1.5, 16.3-16.5): nothing is written unless the output parses,
  re-lexes to the same code (modulo `;`, a `,` before a closer, imports
  compared as a multiset, and parens under `--normalize-parens`), keeps
  every comment in order (a comment travelling with its import is compared
  with it), dumps to the same tree with the compiler's own AST printer, and
  formats to itself. A refused file is left alone and reported; exit 1.
- Section 2: `--no-align`, `--indent=spaces|tabs`, `--tab-size=N`,
  `--normalize-parens`; widths 100 / 80 fixed; blank-line cap 4.
- 4.1: block form (calls, literals, initializers), aligned form
  (parameters, with the block fallback), grid form (imports, list / tuple
  literals), the 80-column limit for calls. 4.2: trailing commas by form.
  4.6: `&&` / `||` chains break before the operator.
- Section 5: 5.1 (and the `{*}` / nested-path forms), 5.2 (block / grid,
  items sorted), 5.3, 5.4 (Scripts/import_lint.py's layout: the run sorts as
  one, lead comments travel). 5.1's module-collision exception as
  import_lint's `module_exists`.
- 6.2 / 6.7 / 11.1-11.4: alignment runs with keyword, name, type and `=`
  columns, `:` glued; named arguments and initializer fields align their
  values when broken; trailing comments in a run align.
- 7.5 one-line type bodies; 8.1 / 8.2 (plus: an if/else chain is one-line
  only as a whole); 8.3; 8.12; 9.5; 12.4; section 13; 3.5.
- The rulings: 8.1 blocks always break; 14.4 the
  blank line after a closer line; 8.2 / 8.4 / 8.11 chained clauses
  (`} case B {`, `} default {`, `} finally {`); 8.3 `if` expressions with
  right-aligned keywords and `try` expressions with `catch` under `try`
  and the `=>` aligned; 8.4 `match` expressions with more than 2 cases
  broken; 8.4 the `case` grid. Golden tests: block_break, blank_after_closer,
  chain_clauses, if_expr, try_expr, match_expr, case_grid.
- 16.1 / 16.2: golden tests in Tests/Formatter (one per rule family, each
  also checked for idempotency; the tree check runs on every input).
- The (proposed) rules kfmt implements each have one switch in
  Extras/Formatter/Options.k (`Rules`).

Not done (copied as written today, as section 15 says):

- 4.6 for operators other than `&&` / `||`; 4.7 chained calls; 6.1 head
  respacing other than the parameter list; 6.3 / 7.1 continuation lines for
  `where` / `requires` / `derives` / `impl`; 6.8 breaking after `= expr`;
  8.5 / 8.8 loop and `eval` headers; 9.7 expression-bodied closures; 11.6.
- https://kairolang.org/docs/toolchain/kfmt/: once the formatter is complete.

Review items found while implementing (not resolved silently):

- 4.2's one-element-tuple clause is moot: Kairo has no one-element tuple
  (`(x,)` is S054E).
- 3.3's example `if  x>0{` -> `if x > 0 {` conflicts with 6.6: a `<` / `>`
  touching a neighbour is copied as written (it may be a generic the parser
  read as a comparison, which a parse-level check cannot see), so `x>0`
  stays. `x==0` does get its spaces.
- 2.3 / 2.4 read literally: in tabs mode a level is `--tab-size` tabs
  (default 1) and a tab counts as `--tab-size` columns, so with the
  default one level counts as 1 column for the width limit.
- 5.4 blank-line barrier: off, as import_lint does (`Rules.
  import_blank_barrier`); a comment that would be orphaned ends the run.
- 11.4: one space past the longest line, as proposed; the house style used
  at least two, so many enum / field comment columns move.
- The grid fills as many columns as fit (at most 8, import_lint's
  grid_rows), so 4.1's two-rows-of-three example would print as one row.
